      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we can complete the square for the terms involving \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression for \\( z \\):\n\n\\[ z = x^2 + 6x + 2y^2 - 4y + 22 \\]\n\nWe will complete the square for the \\( x \\) terms and the \\( y \\) terms separately.\n\n### Completing the Square for \\( x \\):\n\nThe \\( x \\)-terms are \\( x^2 + 6x \\). To complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\( x \\), which is 6, divide by 2 to get 3, and then square it to get 9. So, we can write:\n\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\n### Completing the Square for \\( y \\):\n\nThe \\( y \\)-terms are \\( 2y^2 - 4y \\). To complete the square, we factor out the 2 first:\n\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) \\]\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses. The \\( y \\)-terms inside the parentheses are \\( y^2 - 2y \\). Half of the coefficient of \\( y \\) is -1, and squaring it gives 1. So, we can write:\n\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 \\]\n\nNow, we substitute these completed squares back into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\]\n\nSimplify the constants:\n\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\nThe expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is always non-negative because it is a sum of two squares. The minimum value of \\((x + 3)^2\\) is 0 and the minimum value of \\(2(y - 1)^2\\) is also 0. Therefore, the minimum value of the entire expression occurs when:\n\n\\[ (x + 3)^2 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2(y - 1)^2 = 0 \\]\n\nThis gives:\n\n\\[ x + 3 = 0 \\implies x = -3 \\]\n\\[ y - 1 = 0 \\implies y = 1 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( z \\):\n\n\\[ z = ( -3 + 3)^2 + 2( 1 - 1)^2 + 11 = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11 \\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
