      "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we will complete the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\( x \\)-terms:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x \\]\nTo complete the square, we add and subtract \\((\\frac{6}{2})^2 = 9\\):\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\( y \\)-terms:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y \\]\nTo complete the square, we first factor out the 2:\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) \\]\nInside the parentheses, we complete the square for \\( y^2 - 2y \\):\n\\[ y^2 - 2y = (y - 1)^2 - 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 \\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original function:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\]\nSimplify the constant terms:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\nThe expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is a sum of two squares, which is always non-negative and achieves its minimum value of 0 when both squares are zero. This happens when:\n\\[ x + 3 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y - 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = -3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 1 \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into the function to find the minimum value:\n\\[ z = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\\[ z = (-3 + 3)^2 + 2(1 - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\\[ z = 0^2 + 2 \\cdot 0^2 + 11 \\]\n\\[ z = 11 \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
